      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-222182",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following statement regarding the sets $A$, $B$, and $C$.\n\n$A\u2229(B\u222aC)\u2286(A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $x \u2208 A\u2229(B\u222aC)$\n> $L2$: $\u21d2(x \u2208 A)\u2227(x \u2208 B\u222aC)$\n> $L3$: $\u21d2(x \u2208 A)\u2227((x \u2208 B)\u2228(x \u2208 C))$\n> $L4$: $\u21d2((x \u2208 A)\u2227(x \u2208 B))\u2228((x \u2208 A)\u2227(x \u2208 C))$\n> $L5$: $\u21d2(x \u2208 (A\u2229B))\u2228(x \u2208 (A\u2229C))$\n> $L6$: $\u21d2x \u2208 (A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line $L3$ by {{field-1}}\n\nLine $L5$ follows from line $L4$ by the definition of {{field-2}}\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line $L5$ by the definition of {{field-3}}"
